<p>The Trump administration renewed a limited sanctions waiver that lets certain Russian oil cargoes already loaded onto ships reach their destinations through May 16, after the previous 30-day exemption expired on April 11. Washington appears to have acted as oil prices jumped during the Iran war, aiming to avoid adding pressure to fuel costs and global energy markets while keeping broader pressure on Moscow in place.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/SpZpBfuQA3v1TGgo_PJME2xqcF9jwkhR4sEhd5ktmc1RWBuDm-Ro0RF3mt-C0ehqxgjU5hqko4D3-ZYxcsjGWd1MJ4fNwaCAsV7k8YECrPbNy9mME1gbgYXoGMHzbw2riAK55ncQr2tMVzpaNeamYE_W1dQ7N4z6ZlUK6oE8CGdM-86F3pz7EYy38abDVUyyKFSxh6Bv9_CskUb1ypAcxGC1APKJF6fPMxw2lxL5GeQfk1jmnqNf2WafnfM4q8DbuXa5jrjBTrGjHB6Sug' alt='The MT Desert Kite oil tanker carrying Russian oil at Narara Marine National Park in the Arabian Sea, Gujarat, India, March 11.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Form of relief: NDTV said the extension came through a new U.S. general license, replacing the expired waiver rather than requiring case-by-case approvals.</li><li>Policy reversal: The extension came about two days after U.S. officials publicly said they did not plan to renew it, adding to confusion around Washington&#x27;s sanctions message.</li><li>India exposure: Indian coverage highlighted that continued access to discounted Russian cargoes gives an oil-importing economy more breathing room as Middle East turmoil raises shipping risks and prices.</li><li>Cross-asset signal: The Financial Times reported that U.S. stocks were heading for their best month in six years on hopes the war could ease, showing the waiver arrived during a broader market rebound tied to expectations around the conflict.</li></ul><blockquote>We are remaining in Lebanon in an expanded security zone, - Benjamin Netanyahu</blockquote><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/us-extends-waiver-allowing-countries-to-buy-russian-oil'>US renews Russian oil waiver aft